# Thermal management — Meizu MX6 (m95, MT6797).
#
# The MT6797 has two thermal layers and we were running on only one of them.
#
# The kernel layer works: drivers/misc/mediatek/thermal drives mtktscpu and
# friends and limits CPU power on its own. It is visible in dmesg as
#   [CPU_Thermal]set_adaptive_cpu_power_limit 3791 T=76300, ... T=76800
# Those are SoC self-protection trip points, and they are deliberately high.
#
# The product layer is the MTK thermal daemon set, which reads the tuned
# policies in /vendor/etc/.tp (thermal.conf, thermal.{low,mid,high,off}.conf,
# .thermal_policy_0[0-3], .ht120.mtc) and applies the limits Meizu actually
# shipped, including skin/battery/charger temperature. We install every one of
# those binaries and every one of those policy files (m95-vendor.mk:176-202),
# but no init .rc in our tree ever started any of them - `grep -rn thermal
# los/device/meizu/m95/rootdir/*.rc` returned nothing before this file existed.
# So the device ran to SoC-protection limits only, and got hot in the hand.
#
# Definitions below are copied from the stock ramdisk, init.mt6797.rc:1383-1403
# (extracted from stock/boot.img), including the user/group split - thermald,
# thermal and thermalloadalgod run as system, thermal_manager runs as root and
# is oneshot.
#
# /vendor is a symlink to /system/vendor in our ramdisk, so these paths resolve
# to the files m95-vendor.mk installs under system/vendor/bin.
#
# NOTE for the move to enforcing: these are vendor daemons touching
# /proc/driver/thermal, /sys/class/thermal and the .tp policy files, so they
# will need their own SELinux domains. SELinux is Permissive during bring-up
# (verified with getenforce), so they run today regardless.

# Stock parity (stock init.mt6797.rc:542). libmtcloader persists the active
# policy selection in /data/.tp/.settings; without the directory every
# thermal_manager run logs "mtcloader fails to open .tp.settings and
# exits!errno 2" (FACT 2026-08-14) and falls back to the default policy
# instead of the tuned per-state Meizu policies, and change_policy can
# never persist a selection.
on post-fs-data
    mkdir /data/.tp 0775 system system

service thermalloadalgod /vendor/bin/thermalloadalgod
    class main
    user system
    group system

service thermal_manager /vendor/bin/thermal_manager
    class main
    user root
    group system
    oneshot

service thermal /vendor/bin/thermal
    class main
    user system
    group system

service thermald /vendor/bin/thermald
    class main
    user system
    group system
